Such a hierarchy allows the brain to model the causal structure of its sensory input and form predictions at slower time scales ("someone walking") by representing trajectories capturing the dynamics of its expected spatiotemporal sensory input at different time scales, and by representing causal dependencies between time scales. This allows for inference about the causes of sensory input in the environment, as well as for inference of the brain's own control signals (e.g., motor actions). In this paper, we will review some of the experimental evidence and potential computational models for sequence generation and inference.  

